= Presupposition =

== Overview ==
A '''presupposition''' is information treated as backgrounded or taken for granted rather than presented as the main asserted content.

For example:

<blockquote>Maya stopped smoking.</blockquote>

normally presents Maya's stopping as the main point while treating her previous smoking as backgrounded.

== Triggers ==
Expressions and constructions often associated with presuppositions include change-of-state verbs such as ''stop'', factive verbs such as ''realize'', definite descriptions, clefts, iterative words such as ''again'', and some temporal/aspectual constructions.

Calling something a trigger identifies a recurring pattern; it does not guarantee that every use produces the same presupposition.

== Projection ==
Presuppositions can remain active when the triggering material appears under negation, questions, conditionals, or other operators.

Compare:

<blockquote>Maya stopped smoking.</blockquote>
<blockquote>Maya did not stop smoking.</blockquote>

Both can suggest that Maya had smoked.

How presuppositions project through larger structures is a major theoretical problem.

== Context and accommodation ==
A presupposition may already belong to the [[Common ground|common ground]].

When it does not, listeners may sometimes '''accommodate''' it by updating what is provisionally taken for granted.

Accommodation is context-sensitive and can fail when the backgrounded assumption is implausible, contested, or socially inappropriate.

== Presupposition, entailment, and implicature ==
Presupposition is not identical to logical entailment.

It is also distinct from [[Implicature|implicature]], which concerns additional conveyed content arising through conventional or conversational mechanisms.

Different theories draw these boundaries differently, but collapsing all three loses important behavior.

== Major approaches ==
Presupposition has been analyzed through semantic truth conditions, partial or trivalent semantics, [[Pragmatics|pragmatic]] speaker assumptions, dynamic semantics, discourse update, and probabilistic/experimental models.

